WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.179 --> 00:00:01.179
All right.

00:00:01.179 --> 00:00:03.230
Let's get right into audience questions for
the week.

00:00:03.230 --> 00:00:06.910
I understand what is on people's minds and
that explains why these are the questions

00:00:06.910 --> 00:00:07.910
I've gotten.

00:00:07.910 --> 00:00:11.970
First one is, hey, David, what is the longest
that the United States can realistically stay

00:00:11.970 --> 00:00:14.080
shut down because of coronavirus?

00:00:14.080 --> 00:00:16.209
So I'm going to answer the question as asked.

00:00:16.209 --> 00:00:21.790
But please understand that we never really
shut down to the degree that Europe did.

00:00:21.790 --> 00:00:27.610
And when you look at, you know, 4.8 percent
GDP decline in Q1, that's that's a lot.

00:00:27.610 --> 00:00:28.610
That's crazy.

00:00:28.610 --> 00:00:31.279
But that's an economy that's mostly still
open now.

00:00:31.279 --> 00:00:32.279
OK.

00:00:32.279 --> 00:00:33.660
The shutdowns didn't start until March.

00:00:33.660 --> 00:00:38.559
If we have a 20 percent decline in Q2, April,
May, June.

00:00:38.559 --> 00:00:39.559
That is crazy.

00:00:39.559 --> 00:00:40.730
That's a crazy.

00:00:40.730 --> 00:00:46.559
But consider that that still means 80 percent
of the economy is still up and running.

00:00:46.559 --> 00:00:50.990
So compared to Europe, the United States restrictions
have been really light.

00:00:50.990 --> 00:00:55.880
The stay home orders have been put in place
in a number of states.

00:00:55.880 --> 00:00:58.809
Essential stores are still open for the most
part.

00:00:58.809 --> 00:01:04.040
You know, all grocery stores, hardware stores
in Massachusetts, restaurants remained open

00:01:04.040 --> 00:01:05.220
for takeout.

00:01:05.220 --> 00:01:08.130
People could go around not wearing masks outdoors

00:01:08.130 --> 00:01:10.710
and even indoors for a long time.

00:01:10.710 --> 00:01:12.460
You could go wherever you want.

00:01:12.460 --> 00:01:13.950
Parks were open.

00:01:13.950 --> 00:01:16.820
Some places have had to close parks because
of people gathering.

00:01:16.820 --> 00:01:21.750
A lot of beaches remained open as long as
people weren't on top of each other in Italy.

00:01:21.750 --> 00:01:27.700
Just this week, people started to be allowed
to go more than 200 meters from their house

00:01:27.700 --> 00:01:28.700
or fight.

00:01:28.700 --> 00:01:30.190
I think in Spain it was 500 meters.

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:34.970
We never had that in the United States, even
in the strictest situations.

00:01:34.970 --> 00:01:35.970
Wherever.

00:01:35.970 --> 00:01:39.619
I don't know if we would consider Michigan
to have been the strictest or whatnot, but

00:01:39.619 --> 00:01:42.890
we were never really fully shut down.

00:01:42.890 --> 00:01:50.689
And a lot of what we saw in the U.S. is really
a city vs. suburban suburb rural distinction

00:01:50.689 --> 00:01:57.659
because I've been in Boston, restaurants remained
open for takeout.

00:01:57.659 --> 00:01:59.340
But the streets are quite empty.

00:01:59.340 --> 00:02:01.229
There's like very little traffic going

00:02:01.229 --> 00:02:02.229
places.

00:02:02.229 --> 00:02:10.549
So certainly the more urban areas of the city
seem noticeably empty, but a lot of the basics

00:02:10.549 --> 00:02:12.930
stayed open the entire time.

00:02:12.930 --> 00:02:15.400
Suburbs may not even have been as dramatically
different.

00:02:15.400 --> 00:02:17.959
It's hard for me to say for sure.

00:02:17.959 --> 00:02:21.969
So the answer is in terms of how much longer
can we stay, quote, shut down.

00:02:21.969 --> 00:02:27.669
It's very clear that there is not a lot of
political appetite, even in the more liberal

00:02:27.669 --> 00:02:30.409
states, to stay shut.

00:02:30.409 --> 00:02:34.579
Even a single data longer than public health
officials say that we should.

00:02:34.579 --> 00:02:37.010
So I think we're increasingly going to start
seeing two tiers.

00:02:37.010 --> 00:02:41.699
We're going to start to see places that had
early hotspots and took it very seriously.

00:02:41.699 --> 00:02:46.359
New York and Massachusetts and California
are the ones I'm most familiar with, that

00:02:46.359 --> 00:02:50.750
in the next two weeks to four weeks, two to
three weeks are going to be able to

00:02:50.750 --> 00:02:55.790
start opening some things with caution, still
with masks indoors, outdoors, where when you

00:02:55.790 --> 00:02:57.359
can't social distance.

00:02:57.359 --> 00:02:59.599
But there will be some opening up.

00:02:59.599 --> 00:03:04.569
More than likely, manufacturing hopefully
can start to come back with people wearing

00:03:04.569 --> 00:03:09.439
masks and distancing in of factories, et cetera.

00:03:09.439 --> 00:03:12.150
The governors will sort of figure it out.

00:03:12.150 --> 00:03:18.959
The problem is that we are starting to see
rapidly rising cases in places like Florida,

00:03:18.959 --> 00:03:21.650
Georgia, Texas.

00:03:21.650 --> 00:03:25.859
And there are others, although I don't have
the numbers readily, sort of in my mind and

00:03:25.859 --> 00:03:30.919
on a percentage basis, they are seeing faster
and faster increases in cases.

00:03:30.919 --> 00:03:35.909
They're not going to be able to open up or
if they do, it is going to be significantly

00:03:35.909 --> 00:03:38.120
tragic, significantly tragic.

00:03:38.120 --> 00:03:41.909
So you have some states starting to say we're
going to start running out of unemployment

00:03:41.909 --> 00:03:42.919
insurance money.

00:03:42.919 --> 00:03:45.620
That's going to be pressure, especially for
Republican governors

00:03:45.620 --> 00:03:51.510
who are very concerned with the debt in this
abstract sense to try to open up the saddest

00:03:51.510 --> 00:03:52.559
part about all of it.

00:03:52.559 --> 00:03:55.499
And ultimately, these are decisions that governors
will have to make.

00:03:55.499 --> 00:03:58.369
And the answer is many of them are going to
start opening some things in the next two

00:03:58.369 --> 00:03:59.730
to three weeks.

00:03:59.730 --> 00:04:07.900
Sadly, if on March 5th, when we had over 200
cases, Trump had said from the federal government,

00:04:07.900 --> 00:04:12.450
I need all 50 governors to do a shelter in
place, stay at home.

00:04:12.450 --> 00:04:16.459
After we had done that on March 5th, we would
be in really good shape right now.

00:04:16.459 --> 00:04:18.040
That's what we're seeing in other countries.

00:04:18.040 --> 00:04:22.680
South Korea took care of things despite having
their first case at the same time we did.

00:04:22.680 --> 00:04:27.210
And that is that is the really lost opportunity,
that there is no way to go back and undo at

00:04:27.210 --> 00:04:28.210
this point

00:04:28.210 --> 00:04:28.212
in time.

